== #394 The City of Santa Clarita [organizations] status=live
  body: The City of Santa Clarita was incorporated on 15 December 1987, from the communities of Newhall, Saugus, Valencia and Canyon Country.[1] It is a general-law city, not a charter city: it runs under the state's general laws rather than a charter of its own. Its government is council-manager: five council members, elected to staggered four-year terms, make policy, and a city manager they appoint runs the City's departments.[1] The mayor is not elected by the voters. Each December the council chooses one of its own members to serve a year as mayor and another as Mayor Pro Tem, so the mayoralty rotates.[2] The council was elected at large until 2024; it is now elected by district, from five districts.[5]

On 3 November 1987 the voters of the proposed city approved incorporation, Proposition U, by 14,723 votes to 6,597, and in Proposition V chose to elect the council at large rather than by district, by 11,166 to 7,905. From 26 candidates they elected Howard P. "Buck" McKeon, with 9,855 votes, Janice (Jan) Heidt, 8,402, Jo Anne Darcy, 7,601, Carl Boyer III, 6,585, and Dennis M. Koontz, 6,164. Richard M. Vacar, sometimes listed among the first council, was sixth, with 5,935. The ballots cast numbered 21,918.[3]

The council was elected at large for thirty-five years. A suit under the California Voting Rights Act, filed on 29 December 2021, was settled on 12 April 2022,[4] and Ordinance No. 23-4, adopted on 13 June 2023 by four votes to one, divided the city into five council distric
